  "articleBody": "Getting the rough opening size right for your door is absolutely crucial for a smooth installation. You can't just frame an opening the exact size of your door and expect it to fit! The 'rough opening' needs to be larger than the actual door slab to allow for the door frame, shims, and a little wiggle room for installation. Generally, you'll need to add about 2 inches to the width of your door and 2.5 inches to the height. So, for a standard 30-inch wide door, your rough opening would typically be 32 inches wide. For the height, if your door is 80 inches tall, you'd aim for 82.5 inches. This extra space lets you square and plumb the door unit perfectly within the opening. It’s also super important to plan this all out before you even order your door, because if the framing is wrong, you're going to have a headache. If you're unsure about header sizing, or if the opening is in a load-bearing wall, that's when a professional like HH Remodeling should step in. We can make sure your door opening is perfectly framed, saving you time and frustration.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}